# SWAB(3)

## NAME

swab - swap bytes

## SYNOPSIS

```c
void swab(const char *from, char *to, int nbytes);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Swab* copies *nbytes* bytes pointed to by *from* to the position pointed to by *to,*
exchanging adjacent even and odd bytes. It is useful for carrying binary data between PDP11's
and other machines. *Nbytes* should be even.

**Note:** it is declared by no header - v7 had none and C11 has no such routine - so a caller
declares it itself.

## FILES

| share/besm6/lib/libc.a
| lib/libc/gen/swab.c

## BESM-6 NOTES

**Rewritten over bytes, because there is no 16-bit unit on this machine.**

v7 wrote this loop over **short \***, swapping the halves of a 16-bit word. **short** is an
alias for **int** here - one 48-bit word - so the v7 loop would swap the halves of a *word* and
mean nothing to any caller.

The routine still does what its name and its purpose say: it exchanges adjacent *bytes,*
pairwise, which is what a program reading PDP-11 data needs. An odd *nbytes* leaves the last
byte alone.
